t  soviet politicians were told by western  leaders like german chancellor hamutko  [Music]  nato secretary general manfred warner  [Music]  and the famous words by u.s secretary of  state james baker not one inch to the  east  what happened to nato after germany  unified  in 1999 the czech republic hungary and  poland joined nato  in 2004 it was the baltic states and  four other countries  and finally four more from 2009 to just  a few years ago george and ukraine were  promised membership in 2008 but are not  yet members  this seems to justify putin's grievances  but one historical event could suggest a  different interpretation  back in 1991 in the collapse of the  soviet union  this is stephen pifer he was the u.s  ambassador to ukraine when nato was  expanding and deputy director on the  soviet desk when those assurances were  made when secretary baker at that time  was talking about nato not extending  forward  i understood that it's not into east  germany  pifer says that those commitments didn't  refer to countries like poland or  lithuania because at that time also i  mean the warsaw pact still existed the  war so i didn't collapse for another  year  so i saw the discussions about not one  inch really in the context of germany  many experts disagree that doesn't quite  hold water uh for two different reasons  one is that the soviet leadership and  the americans both understood that the  question of what comes next in europe  was all about the future of eastern  europe not just east germany there's a  concern the warsaw pact is falling apart  and also if i say to you i'm not going  east  uh it's a pretty generic statement right  it kind of biggers the imagination to  think that they only meant oh east only  there but no further from there  however raises another point when you  look at the actual agreements you know  there were there were no final  commitments by nato not to enlarge in  other words no treaties only  conversations  nothing was written down as we've  established but at the same time  political understandings and commitments  of the sort of diplomatic commitments  carry a lot of water in oral policy this  is how we ended the cuban missile crisis  for example  uh and if it didn't matter why would we  have diplomats talk to each other  regardless of how serious these  assurances were this debate arguably  misses a more important point  states don't uphold  treaties just because they're written  down if those leaders in those states  decide to change course then a treaty is  not going to stand in the way  so all the emphasis on writing things  down  you know it puts the carpet for the  horse  nato's expansion can be better explained  by focusing not on those conversations  but on the countries involved  [Music]  on one side the us whose influence grew  after the fall of the soviet union nato  expansion was fundamentally a byproduct  of the unipolar era right at this moment  in time where the u.s had great power  very few constraints and at least some  policymakers who had an idea that  american power and influence apply to  the world would generate lots of goods  for the united states as well as good  things for the world  on another the countries that joined  nato  it wasn't that nato said let's enlarge  it was you had countries such as poland  emerging from the records of the warsaw  pact saying  we want to be a member of nato we want  to be a member of the european union and  we are prepared to do  all of the things that requires  and finally russia whose motivations are  not limited to nato i think there are  really three reasons behind what russia  is doing one is the geopolitical concern  the russians want a sphere of influence  in the post-soviet space and ukraine is  a very important part of that so they're  trying to bring it back but there are  two other issues at play here one i  think is a domestic political concern  and that is for the kremlin  a western oriented  democratic  economically successful that kind of  ukraine is a nightmare for the kremlin  because it leads to russians asking why  can't we have the same political voice  and the same democratic rights that they  have in ukraine and then there's a third  risk which i just think is it's  is vladimir putin and he's quite  emotional about ukraine he's very  emotional and even angry about it  whether nato should have kept its  assurances is up for debate we  underestimated just how much antipathy  there was in moscow to nato's existence  years of the us downplaying the  assurance that were previously given and  making russia feel like it could not  trust the west have really narrowed the  opportunity to resolve this crisis  but it's really sort of a pretext mr  putin has put together this list of  grievances against the west you know but  he didn't talk about them 20 years ago  but those discussions were important not  only for what they promised  but also for what they showed  a willingness to compromise and to not  cross each other red lines  [Music]  over the years the situation around  russia changed and more countries  decided to join nato  but it changed even more within russia  as putin's grip on power strengthened  and he became more authoritarian  [Music]  and isolated
 

